Crank-Log

Ingredients: HURDY-GURDY + AMAPIANO + PREPARED PIANO + BASS CLARINET + TAPE DUB + 2020s ERA

Creative Catalyst: The genre began when a hurdy-gurdy player rehearsing inside a disused tram maintenance depot discovered that the instrument’s buzzing bridge could lock unnervingly well with the pitch contour of an amapiano log drum. A producer mapped crank speed to the rhythmic density of a log-drum bassline, while a pianist prepared an upright piano with felt, rubber, and loose metal washers so its attack could live between wheel friction and club percussion.

Crank-Log is a low-pressure dance form built around continuous mechanical motion. The hurdy-gurdy is the central engine: its wheel-driven drone provides a persistent tonal floor, its melody strings supply narrow modal figures, and its chien buzzing bridge becomes a percussive trigger rather than a historical curiosity. Amapiano contributes the deep, elastic log-drum vocabulary, but the style avoids bright party gloss; the bass is sparse, pitched, and conversational, entering in short phrases that answer the crank rather than dominating every bar.

Prepared piano occupies the middle register. Felted hammers, rubber wedges, muted strings, and occasional metal preparations create dry woody attacks, short metallic after-rings, and unstable sympathetic resonance. Bass clarinet supplies the breath layer, moving slowly beneath the crank texture with low-register swells, key clicks, and restrained multiphonics. Tape-dub processing ties the ensemble together through hard mutes, dark feedback, and delayed fragments that appear after the source has already disappeared.

The defining tension is between rotation and drop. The hurdy-gurdy wheel suggests endless continuity; amapiano’s log drum keeps interrupting that continuity with downward-pitched gestures, pauses, and delayed arrivals. Crank-Log therefore feels like a machine that never stops turning even when the floor beneath it keeps changing level.

Genre DNA
* Tempo & Pulse: 110–116 BPM, usually 4/4 with laid-back amapiano swing. Upper percussion uses restrained shakers, dry claps, and occasional 3+3+2 accent cells. The hurdy-gurdy crank remains mechanically steady while melody phrases and log-drum entries drift around the grid.
* Harmonic Palette: Drone-centered Dorian, Aeolian, and Mixolydian fields; open fifths, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, and slowly changing bass pedals. Chord progression is secondary to timbral movement and overtone interaction.
* Production Signatures: 1. Crank-Follower Gate — a transient/envelope follower derived from wheel friction opens short filtered-noise or percussion layers; 2. Log-Drone Counterweight — log-drum notes are selected to create contrary motion against the hurdy-gurdy drone rather than simply reinforce roots; 3. Felt-Rattle Piano — prepared-piano attacks are split into dry transient and resonant tail buses, allowing the resonance to be dub-muted independently; 4. Chien-Sidechain — the buzzing bridge briefly ducks the upper mids of the log drum and bass clarinet so each buzz reads as a structural accent; 5. Depot Echo Matrix — tape delays modeled on long concrete service bays return only selected phrases, often after one or two bars of silence.
* Dynamics: Tracks grow through density and spatial depth, not through loudness. Early sections may contain only drone, one piano attack, and sparse rim percussion. Peak sections add interlocking log-drum phrases, buzzing-bridge accents, bass-clarinet counterlines, and wider dub feedback. Breakdowns remove the electronic grid while the hurdy-gurdy wheel continues uninterrupted.

Scene & Origin Lore

Crank-Log formed around a closed tram maintenance depot in Bratislava that had been temporarily converted into a workshop for instrument repair, sound art, and community rehearsal. The building contained inspection pits, long concrete service lanes, suspended work lamps, heavy steel doors, and several decommissioned wheel-lathes whose bearings still clicked when turned by hand.

Hurdy-gurdy player Lenka Varga first noticed that the rhythm of the instrument’s buzzing bridge resembled the stuttering low-end phrasing of an amapiano set being played in an adjacent room. Johannesburg-born producer Kabelo Maseko recorded the wheel friction, buzzing bridge, bearing clicks, and service-bay footsteps, then rebuilt them into a minimal percussion system. Rather than placing a generic log-drum pattern beneath the folk instrument, he tuned each bass gesture to destabilize or clarify the hurdy-gurdy drone at specific moments.

Pianist Marek Holub prepared an upright piano using maintenance materials found on site: felt strips, rubber gaskets, washers, cardboard shims, and sections of brake lining. Bass clarinetist Ilse Novak joined later, using long breath phrases to smooth the sharp boundary between wooden wheel noise and sub-bass.

Their first performances were called rotation tests. The hurdy-gurdy player began alone and was required to keep the wheel moving from the first sound to the last. Other musicians entered only when they could hear a repeating mechanical relationship in the room: a bearing click, an HVAC relay, a swinging cable, or a distant door. The ensemble adopted this rule permanently. No track may begin with the full rhythm section, and no track may end with the wheel stopping abruptly.

