Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Wheel Keeps Turning After Shift
- Story & Context: During a late rehearsal, Lenka notices that one old wheel-lathe bearing in the depot continues rotating for nearly a minute after being pushed. She starts the hurdy-gurdy wheel at the same rate and keeps it there while Kabelo records the bearing clicks. The log drum enters only when the bearing begins slowing, creating a descending bass phrase that seems to pull against the stable drone. Marek answers with single prepared-piano notes damped almost immediately after attack, while Ilse holds a low bass-clarinet tone just below the drone’s strongest overtone. The arrangement becomes denser as the bearing slows, reversing the expected relationship between motion and intensity. In the final section, the bearing stops but Lenka keeps the hurdy-gurdy wheel moving alone for another thirty-two bars.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close cinematic view of an old tram wheel-lathe bearing still turning in a dark maintenance depot after work hours, hurdy-gurdy wheel in the foreground matching its rotation, chalk timing marks on concrete, one hanging utility lamp, copper cable and grease stains, realistic industrial documentary photography. Spoken fragments limited to “still moving,” “same speed,” and “do not stop with it.”

Track 2: Log Drum Under Bay Seven
- Story & Context: Kabelo discovers that the inspection pit beneath Bay Seven reinforces one narrow low-frequency band. Instead of correcting the room response, he tunes a three-note log-drum phrase so only the final note excites the pit strongly. Lenka sustains one drone and uses the chien buzz only on the bars where that third bass note appears. Marek places felt between several piano hammers and strings, producing dull attacks that resemble maintenance mallets striking rubber. Ilse stands inside the empty pit and plays short bass-clarinet tones upward into the room, allowing the concrete cavity to become a second resonator. Halfway through the track, the ensemble removes the strongest log-drum note entirely; listeners continue anticipating the physical resonance even though it no longer arrives. The final chorus restores it once, then never repeats it.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Deep concrete tram inspection pit beneath a numbered Bay Seven sign, compact subwoofer and measurement microphone at floor level, hurdy-gurdy case beside the edge, bass clarinet player visible below, prepared upright piano under a distant work lamp. Lyrics and spoken text should use maintenance language: “third note only,” “mark the bay,” “leave the pit open.”

Track 3: Felt Washer on the C-Sharp
- Story & Context: Marek finds that a loose metal washer placed on one prepared-piano string creates a faint rattle only when a particular C-sharp is struck softly. He attempts to repeat it at performance volume, but the vibration throws the washer off the string. The band decides to build the track around the quieter dynamic instead of stabilizing the preparation. Kabelo reduces the drum pattern to one dry kick, one shaker cell, and a two-note log-drum answer. Lenka uses almost no melody, relying on continuous drone and subtle wheel-pressure changes. Ilse records key clicks separately and feeds them into a dark tape delay that returns after the original piano note has faded. The climax is intentionally quiet: the washer rattles correctly for several seconds while every electronic element drops out. The take ends when the washer finally falls onto the piano frame.
